{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nsources=['S_COMPLETION_EXPECTATION','S_STABLE_EXPECTATION','S_API','S_CONFIG','S_STORAGE_MSG','S_DISPATCH','S_UNSTABLE','S_INIT','S_MEMORY_SNAPSHOT','S_MEMORY_HARDSTATE','S_MEMORY_APPEND','S_CAMPAIGN','S_ROLE','S_READY_ACK','S_LOG_APPLY']\nreview=dict(aspect='checker_correspondence',status='no_issue_found',source_ids=sources,rationale='Reviewed the fixed plan and assurance_generated_test.go for artifact 30b99642cd6d4bb89f9e66b2d66afa77 version 1, its C_STALE_APPEND_COMPLETION version 1 basis, and raw execution e79e31386cd248c8bacd4ffbecbac979 plus parsed correlations. The requirement comes from S_COMPLETION_EXPECTATION, not from the observed guard: an old raft-term append completion must not truncate unstable entries. S_STABLE_EXPECTATION explains why entry identity alone is insufficient. RawNode is the actual selected entry and all calls are serialized. The prefix is an explicitly supplied consistent snapshot/HardState at index/term 1 with voters 1/2/3; the test does not claim to execute that prefix production. After construction, actual Campaign, Propose, Ready, MemoryStorage writes and Step execute the history without assigning protocol state or fabricating responses. The append worker processes each node work in Ready order, saves entries and nonempty HardState before releasing responses, and finitely holds only node 3 MsgStorageAppendResp. Those held messages are later consumed unchanged and in production order. Modeled storage completion means successful MemoryStorage calls in a noncrashing execution, not disk durability. Normal-entry no-op application and omission of ticks do not interfere with the selected synchronous completion consumer: there are no application-dependent configuration entries, elections are explicitly driven, and CheckQuorum is disabled.\\\\n\\\\nIdentity is (node, marker_id), assigned from held-list position at producer emission and retained by the same immutable pending sequence at consumption. It is independent of response term/index and unstable offset, so repeated marker tuples remain distinct. The origin event occurs after storage processing; the before event occurs immediately before the specific Step call and references origin.response_term as a predecessor constraint. The completed event follows Step return unconditionally. Raw terms and entry identity are emitted; stale is computed before Step as response.Term < raft.Term, independently of the offset comparison. All five marker identities have one origin, one admission and one completion. The monitor selected before as admission_alias and correlated events 9/11/13/15/17 to their respective origins and admissions, with no parsing errors or missing results.\\\\n\\\\nFor markers 0 and 1, response term 2 is below receiving term 3 and entry (3,2) still matches. Both comparisons observe unstable offset 3 before and after Step. The current-term marker 2 from a zero-entry append has the same entry identity and advances offset 3 to 4; marker 3 advances 4 to 5, and duplicate marker 4 leaves it 5. These latter operations are permitted controls, not required-success instances: event_implication applies the equality assertion only when stale is true. The independent expected relation is offset_after = before.unstable_offset, directly encoding no prefix truncation because stableTo removes prefix entries by advancing offset. No event interleaves within Step, and no snapshot is present to introduce an independent completion effect. The final pump observes applied=committed=4 and no held markers; final application is diagnostic, whereas synchronous Step completion is the required endpoint.\\\\n\\\\nThis supports the runner bounded holds measurement for the two stale completion instances and its complete per-operation correlation. The result answers local context-fence consumption in this history, not the broader causal hypothesis about ABA overwrites or consensus divergence. No correspondence issue was found within this fixed scope; that review judgment is not an overall proof.',counterevidence=['The implementation already has an explicit raft-term filter and an additional entry index/log-term guard; the execution is consistent with these protections, not evidence of a defect.','Old-term snapshot completion is intentionally handled even when entry completion is rejected; all checked responses are snapshot-free.','Fresh-term completion for an old entry term is permitted and observed, so treating entry term as the invalidating context would be wrong.'],limitations=['One three-node, two-election, no-replacement execution checks two stale markers; it does not universally quantify schedules or configurations.','The initial committed snapshot prefix is supplied consistently, not generated from process birth.','MemoryStorage is a noncrashing storage substitute; no physical durability, pending-write overwrite or restart endpoint is observed.','Prefix-truncation observation uses unstable.offset; this check does not assert arbitrary payload/state preservation.','Application payload effects, snapshots, membership changes and client-visible consensus consequences remain excluded.'])\ns=dict(action='review',artifact_id='30b99642cd6d4bb89f9e66b2d66afa77',rationale='Review the executed fixed check as a whole against its accepted local responsibility, actual producer history and per-marker completed comparisons.',review_items=[review],feedback=dict(ref_ids=['e79e31386cd248c8bacd4ffbecbac979','S_COMPLETION_EXPECTATION','S_STORAGE_MSG','S_DISPATCH','S_READ_QUEUE','S_ACTIVATE','S_ACK'],answered='The fixed direct check measured no prefix truncation for both stale-term marker consumptions, with five of five admitted markers correlated to completed Step calls. Fresh-term controls stabilized entries and the final pump reached applied=commit=4. Correspondence review found no issue for this local, noncrashing, snapshot-free, no-replacement scope. Wider overwrite or consensus consequences are not inferred.',remaining=['Safe-read acknowledgement eligibility when the active voter configuration changes remains a distinct untested relationship.','An ABA/replacement schedule could test the wider storage-view consequence, but the measured local term fence does not require that consequence.','Synchronous Ready ordering and ApplyConfChange cancellation wording remain separate contract surfaces.'],understanding='unchanged',rationale='Return to the accepted overview: the storage term fence now has a bounded measured result and explicit protections. Compare a replacement-history extension (stronger consequence but requires carefully correlated in-flight storage writes), safe-read support across configuration activation (potential read-safety consequence with a distinct quorum reinterpretation), and synchronous documentation reconciliation (harness legality rather than a new safety endpoint). Prefer source expansion of the safe-read producer/consumer and configuration transition relationship next, because the local stale-completion question is answered for its selected schedule while read support is an uncovered core relationship.',question_updates={'138bfd8816444e7d9e6e615ac2d3fd2a':dict(unknowns=['Behavior under other schedules, replacement/ABA histories, snapshots and crash recovery is not established by this local check.','No end-to-end consensus or client-visible consequence follows from the bounded no-truncation result.'],resume_conditions=[])}))\nPath('submission-review.json').write_text(json.dumps(s,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\nPY\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess\nr=json.load(open('../research.json'));p=subprocess.run(r['validation']['command']+['submission-review.json'],capture_output=True,text=True);print(p.stdout);print(p.stderr)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
